  "icon": "CalendarClock",
  "estMinutes": 12,
  "title": "Issue the interim payment certificate and get paid on it",
  "desc": "Measure the period against the bill, value it at the contract rates, hold retention, get the principal agent to issue the interim payment certificate and open the clock on the certificate date, because the window that follows it is measured in days rather than weeks.",
  "longDesc": "The distinctive thing about this payment regime is how little time it leaves. The principal agent issues an interim payment certificate and the employer pays within a handful of calendar days of the certificate date, which means the certificate is the event that matters and the invoice is administration behind it. Firms that run the clock from their own invoice date consistently discover they were already late, because the days were counting while the paperwork moved internally. Opening the clock on the certificate, and watching it rather than filing it, is the whole difference between a payment that arrives and a default the contract has a remedy for.",
